ing for word problem; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Mixing collection problems with part-of-whole addition without noticing the structure causes wrong operations.. Correction uses (a) 4 stickers (b) 4/5 of the ribbon.. Why: restores accurate word problem.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Mixing collection problems with part-of-whole addition without noticing the structure causes wrong operations. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses word problem with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with word problem, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for word problem, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about word problem, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for word problem. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","(a) 4 stickers (b) 4/5 of the ribbon","Mixing collection problems with part-of-whole addition without noticing the structure causes wrong operations","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Solve: (a) 1/4 of 16 stickers (b) 3/5 + 1/5 of a ribbon (same whole). Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for word problem; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of fraction and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Mixing collection problems with part-of-whole addition without noticing the structure causes wrong operations","Fraction word problems may ask for a fraction of a collection or for joining/removing equal parts of the same whole","(a) 4 stickers (b) 4/5 of the ribbon"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named fraction and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name fraction and explain why it matters only, without linking it to word problem","Apply word problem to new material and name the fraction and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Mixing collection problems with part-of-whole addition without noticing the structure causes wrong operations"],"question":"Which response best applies word problem with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid","Write only a final number with no working","Change digits to look neater without checking place value","Mixing collection problems with part-of-whole addition without noticing the structure causes wrong operations"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on word problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught word problem method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Read 1