A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Fraction word problems may ask for a fraction of a collection or for joining/removing equal parts of the same whole"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about word problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"Mia ate 2/8 of a pizza and later 3/8 of the same pizza\" → 5/8 eaten. \"1/3 of 12 apples\" → 4 apples.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve: (a) 1/4 of 16 stickers (b) 3/5 + 1/5 of a ribbon (same whole).\n\nFEEDBACK\n(a) 4 stickers (b) 4/5 of the ribbon.","explain":"Fraction word problems may ask for a fraction of a collection or for joining/removing equal parts of the same whole.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Mixing collection problems with part-of-whole addition without noticing the structure causes wrong operations. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Collection fraction contrasted with part-of-whole join.","caption":"Structure chooses the method.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=