at each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Same-whole fraction addition in a story.","caption":"Name the whole in the answer.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Ribbon</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">3/5 + 1/5</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Result</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">4/5</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about word problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMixing collection problems with part-of-whole addition without noticing the structure causes wrong operations. Here is a more productive model: \"Mia ate 2/8 of a pizza and later 3/8 of the same pizza\" → 5/8 eaten. \"1/3 of 12 apples\" → 4 apples.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n(a) 4 stickers (b) 4/5 of the ribbon.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Mixing collection problems with part-of-whole addition without noticing the structure causes wrong operations. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea a